55,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option B and D both offer shared access, which the data suggests leads to higher satisfaction. However, Option B addresses the critical shortage of general-purpose rooms and provides flexible, bookable spaces for all.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Option B seems promising for utility, but we must consider the disruption. Option D also offers shared access, yet we need to know which plan minimizes the need for off-site space during construction.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,George,,"Option A is the only choice that directly addresses the operational strain. Upgrading the extraction equipment would eliminate the manual routines currently burdening our technicians, providing them with much-needed relief.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A is a necessity driven by infrastructure, making it the most likely to stay on schedule. Options B, C, and D are based on appeal and are high-risk for the delays I've observed.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option A is a necessity, yet its reliance on mechanical plant makes it a financial gamble given current market trends. Option B is a safer, more modest investment in utility.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"While Option A addresses operational needs, it risks the financial instability Benjamin mentioned. Option B directly solves the demand for general-purpose rooms and aligns with the high satisfaction ratings associated with shared-access spaces.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"Option B converts an underused hall, which likely minimizes the need to take active teaching rooms out of service. This avoids the costly off-site hiring and timetable disruptions that have plagued us in the past.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,George,,"Financial risks are a concern, but the human cost of inaction is higher. If we ignore the technicians' burden, we risk a total operational failure. Option A is the only plan that provides essential relief.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"I understand the human cost, George, but Option A's complexity often leads to the very budget and schedule slips I fear. Option B is a simpler conversion with a much lower risk of timeline collapse.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is refreshing to see such a spirited debate. While George's concern for the staff is noble, Option A's mechanical complexity is a recipe for budget overruns. Option B is far more fiscally disciplined and predictable.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"Option B aligns with the data on user satisfaction and the urgent need for general-purpose spaces. It is the most balanced choice, satisfying utility, budget, and scheduling constraints while benefiting the widest range of users.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree. Option B maximizes utility and shared access while minimizing the risk of timetable disruption and expensive off-site hiring. It is the most rational choice given the constraints we have identified.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,George,,"I cannot agree. We are prioritizing convenience and aesthetics over the well-being of the staff. If we do not address the manual burden in the labs now, we are simply neglecting the people who sustain this university.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"I sympathize with the staff, but we cannot ignore the pattern of failure. Option B is a low-risk conversion. Option A is a complex project that could easily slip and leave us in chaos.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we have a clear divide between the pragmatic and the idealistic. While the labs are important, Option B avoids the volatile plant market and the risk of a financial black hole. I shall vote for B.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"Option B is the only proposal that addresses the systemic failure of our space allocation. It transforms an underused area into a high-utility resource, ensuring the greatest benefit for the most people. I vote for B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Victoria,,Option B is the most responsible choice. It provides high utility and shared access while avoiding the costly timetable disruptions and off-site hiring associated with other projects. I vote for B.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,George,,I cannot in good conscience support a plan that ignores the exhaustion of our staff. We must prioritize the people over the furniture. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I appreciate George's concern for the staff, but we must be realistic. Option B is the most stable choice, avoiding the risks of schedule slippage and budget overruns. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"The consensus is quite overwhelming. While I admire George's tenacity, I cannot ignore the financial volatility of mechanical plant costs. For the sake of our budget's survival, I also vote for Option B.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
